{
  "moduleId": "m00263",
  "title": {
    "segmentId": "m00263:title:auto-1",
    "text": "Alkylation of Enolate Ions"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "49c3bbaf-f2e8-4cc1-bd1e-582e0f446a96"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00001",
      "segmentId": "m00263:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00001",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00263:alt:fig-00001-alt",
          "text": "A carbonyl compound reacts with a base to form an enolate ion which undergoes an S N 2 reaction with R 3 C X to form a carbonyl compound with an R 3 C group attached."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_22_07_001.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00002",
      "segmentId": "m00263:para:para-00002"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00002",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00263:alt:fig-00002-alt",
          "text": "Comparative rate of reaction of R-X molecules with an enolate. For X, fastest to slowest is tosylate, iodide, bromide, and chloride. For R, fastest to slowest is allylic and benzylic, then primary alkyl and then secondary alkyl."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_22_07_002.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00001",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00263:title:sect-00001-title",
        "text": "The Malonic Ester Synthesis"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00003",
          "segmentId": "m00263:para:para-00003"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00003",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00263:alt:fig-00003-alt",
              "text": "An alkyl halide (R-X) undergoes malonic ester synthesis to form carboxylic acid. In the product, an R group is attached to a C H 2 group which in turn is attached to a C O O H group."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_22_07_003.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00004",
          "segmentId": "m00263:para:para-00004"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00004",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00263:alt:fig-00004-alt",
              "text": "Diethyl propanedioate reacts with sodium ethoxide in ethanol to form an intermediate sodio malonic ester, which reacts with R X to form an alkylated malonic ester as the product."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_22_07_004.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00005",
          "segmentId": "m00263:para:para-00005"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00005",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00263:alt:fig-00005-alt",
              "text": "An alkylated malonic ester reacts with sodium ethoxide in ethanol to form an intermediate, which reacts with R dash X to form a dialkylated malonic ester as the product."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_22_07_005.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00006",
          "segmentId": "m00263:para:para-00006"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00006",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00263:alt:fig-00006-alt",
              "text": "When heated, an alkylated malonic ester is protonated to form a carboxylic acid, carbon dioxide, and 2 equivalents of ethanol."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_22_07_006.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00007",
          "segmentId": "m00263:para:para-00007"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00007",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00263:alt:fig-00007-alt",
              "text": "A diacid undergoes decarboxylation to form an acid enol, which, in turn, forms a carboxylic acid as the product. A beta-keto acid undergoes decarboxylation to form an enol which tautomerizes to a ketone."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_22_07_007.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00008",
          "segmentId": "m00263:para:para-00008"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00008",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00263:alt:fig-00008-alt",
              "text": "1-Bromobutane and diethyl malonate react with sodium ethoxide to give hexanoic acid via an intermediate. The intermediate can also react with sodium ethoxide and then methyl iodide to yield 2-methyl hexanoic acid."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_22_07_008.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00009",
          "segmentId": "m00263:para:para-00009"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00009",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00263:alt:fig-00009-alt",
              "text": "1,4-Dibromobutane and diethyl malonate react with sodium ethoxide to form cyclopentane-carboxylic acid via three intermediates."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_22_07_009.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "id": "exam-00001",
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00263:example-title:exam-00001-title",
            "text": "Using Malonic Ester Synthesis to Prepare a Carboxylic Acid"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00010",
              "segmentId": "m00263:para:para-00010"
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00011",
              "segmentId": "m00263:para:para-00011",
              "title": {
                "segmentId": "m00263:para-title:para-00011-title",
                "text": "Strategy"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00012",
              "title": {
                "segmentId": "m00263:para-title:para-00012-title",
                "text": "Solution"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00010",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00263:alt:fig-00010-alt",
              "text": "1-Bromopentane and diethyl malonate react with sodium ethoxide in the first step and on heating with acid in the second step to yield 1-heptanoic acid."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_22_07_010.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
