      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00263:title:sect-00007-title",
        "text": "Biological Alkylations"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00034",
          "segmentId": "m00263:para:para-00034"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00020",
          "class": "scaled-down",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00263:caption:fig-00020-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00263:alt:fig-00020-alt",
              "text": "Indolylpyruvate reacts with base in the presence of S-adenosylmethionine to form two intermediates, eventually yielding indolmycin, an antibiotic."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_22_07_028.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00008",
      "class": "chemistry-matters",
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00263:title:sect-00008-title",
        "text": "Barbiturates"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00035",
          "segmentId": "m00263:para:para-00035"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00021",
          "class": "scaled-down",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00263:caption:fig-00021-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00263:alt:fig-00021-alt",
              "text": "An image displays different colorful barbiturates as tablets, pills, and capsules."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_22_98_BarbColors.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00036",
          "segmentId": "m00263:para:para-00036"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00022",
          "class": "scaled-down",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00263:caption:fig-00022-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00263:alt:fig-00022-alt",
              "text": "Diethyl malonate reacts with three different sets of reactants to form three intermediates that each further react with urea and sodium ethoxide to yield three products named amobarbital, pentobarbital, and secobarbital."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_22_07_031.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00023",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00263:alt:fig-00023-alt",
              "text": "Structure of Barbital (Veronal) is a six-membered ring comprising of carbonyl groups at positions1, 3, and 5, N H groups at positions 2 and 6, and a carbon at position 4 bearing two ethyl groups."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_22_07_029.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00037",
          "segmentId": "m00263:para:para-00037"
        }
      ]
